✅ 17. Synchronize a Block

17.1 Definition

🔴 A synchronized block synchronizes only a specific section of code instead of the entire method.
🔴 Only one thread at a time can execute that block for the specified monitor object.

17.2 Syntax:

synchronized(lockObject) {
    // Critical Section
}

17.3 Why Do We Need Synchronized Blocks?

Suppose you have this method:

public synchronized void processOrder() {
    validateOrder();
    updateDatabase();
    sendEmail();
}

Question:

🔴 Do all three operations need synchronization?
validateOrder()      ❌ No
updateDatabase()     ✅ Yes
sendEmail()          ❌ No

Synchronizing the entire method unnecessarily blocks other threads.

Instead:

public void processOrder() {
    validateOrder();
    synchronized(this) {
        updateDatabase();
    }
    sendEmail();
}

Now only the critical section is protected.

17.4 Syntax

synchronized(object){
    // Critical Section
}

17.5. How It Works Internally

Suppose

synchronized(lock){
    count++;
}

Flow

Acquire Lock
↓
Execute Critical Section
↓
Release Lock

Everything outside the block executes normally without acquiring the monitor.

17.6 Possible Output

Thread-A Doing Some Work
Thread-B Doing Some Work

Thread-A Count = 1
Thread-A Finished

Thread-B Count = 2
Thread-B Finished

Thread-A Doing Some Work
Thread-A Count = 3

Notice:

The "Doing Some Work" messages may interleave because they are outside the synchronized block.
But the increment operation remains thread-safe.

17.7 Internal Flow

Thread-A

Normal Code
↓
Acquire Lock
↓
Critical Section
↓
Release Lock
↓
Normal Code

Thread-B

Normal Code
↓
BLOCKED
↓
Acquire Lock
↓
Critical Section
↓
Release Lock

17.8 Why Use Synchronized Block Instead of Method?

Suppose

public synchronized void process() {
    calculate();
    save();
    print();
}

Entire method locked.

Even
calculate();
which doesn't need synchronization.

Instead

public void process() {
    calculate();
    synchronized(this){
        save();
    }
    print();
}

Only
save();
is protected.
This improves concurrency.


